  "member_id": "el-manira-abdel",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Douglas R. Lowy",
          "Ian H. Frazer",
          "John T. Schiller"
        ],
        "citation": "for virus-like particle vaccines that prevent HPV infection and related cancers"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B010",
    "B029"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B004 most completely joins a defined virological and vaccine advance to mature, durable prevention of infection and major cancers. Relative to the alternatives, its medical consequence is exceptional and its proposed three-person allocation is comparatively coherent, provided that the award is confined to the VLP vaccine advance.",
    "case_against": "The historical chain includes papillomavirus biology, VLP research, clinical development, and implementation. The citation must not absorb those broader contributions or duplicate prior recognition of HPV-cancer causation. B010 remains a formidable alternative because it links physiological discovery to major therapeutic transformation, but its proposed allocation spans a wider discovery-to-therapy pathway.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius favors GLP-1 physiology and therapeutic application, while questioning whether HPV vaccine recognition can be adequately distinguished from prior recognition of HPV causation.",
        "response": "The qualification is decisive for wording but does not erase the distinct achievement. Prophylactic VLP vaccination established an effective preventive intervention against infection and cancer; it is not an award for establishing viral causation. B010 has comparably mature clinical importance, but the supplied record leaves greater uncertainty about allocating linked physiological, receptor, and pharmaceutical contributions to three people."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann argues that the VLP vaccine principle and its preventive result provide a more delimited discovery-to-medicine narrative than GLP-1, while requiring careful scrutiny of the credited trio.",
        "response": "I agree. The strongest justification for B004 is not general public-health magnitude alone, but the combination of a specific VLP vaccine achievement, long-established preventive effect, and a defensible compact allocation. The remaining attribution concern warrants narrow citation language rather than expansion to clinical development or implementation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "svenningsson-per",
        "point": "Svenningsson emphasizes that B029 offers exceptional conceptual depth and clear complementary attribution to Miller and Cooper.",
        "response": "B029 is an especially strong foundational alternative, with an attractive two-person allocation. I nevertheless retain B004 because the VLP advance has yielded unusually direct and sustained prevention of major human cancers while remaining sufficiently distinct if precisely delimited."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller most completely capture the foundational VLP vaccine contribution within the three-person limit.",
      "Whether the final wording can cleanly distinguish VLP vaccination from prior recognition of HPV-cancer causation and from later clinical development and implementation.",
      "Whether B010's broader endocrine and therapeutic transformation should outweigh B004 despite its more diffuse attribution."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-related connection is apparent from the supplied profile. I disclose prior coauthored work with committee member Thomas Perlmann; no candidate-related conflict is documented."
  }
}
